<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="60–75 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Grammar · Lesson 1</p>
  <h1>Third & mixed conditionals: <em>rebuild the timeline without pretending it happened</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">Counterfactual reasoning compares the known world with an unreal alternative. A Third Conditional keeps the alternative cause and consequence in the past. A Mixed Conditional connects different times because an earlier choice still matters now, or because an ongoing characteristic helps explain an earlier outcome. The grammar maps the imagined relationship. It does not prove that one change would certainly have produced the claimed result.</p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">60–75 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">past alternative · past result</span>
    <span class="tag">mixed time · certainty · evidence</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min listening and retrieval</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Reconstruct</b><span>Separate what actually happened from the unreal alternative.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Map time</b><span>Locate the condition and consequence independently.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Calibrate</b><span>Choose would, could, or might without overstating evidence.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Review</b><span>Deliver a fair counterfactual analysis and defend its limits.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line"><b>Can-do:</b> By the end, you can distinguish actual events from unreal alternatives; build positive, negative, and question forms; use a Third Conditional for past-to-past relationships; connect a past cause to a present or future consequence; connect a current or continuing characteristic to a past consequence; choose <span class="ex">would, could</span>, or <span class="ex">might</span> for expected consequence, available possibility, or uncertainty; recognize formal conditional inversion; hear common U.S. contractions and reductions; evaluate a fictional project without turning hindsight into proof or blame; and defend a measured counterfactual recommendation.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You recovered remote present framing, the Past Perfect, a past participle, and the difference between activity and completed result.">
    <h3>Reactivate the time tools</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Conditional prerequisite choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="If I had more time, I would study data analysis">If I had more time, I would study data analysis</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="had completed the mobile test">had completed the mobile test</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="written">written</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="had tested twelve devices">had tested twelve devices</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="would had completed">would had completed</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Use a Second Conditional for a different present situation: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="If I had more time, I would study data analysis" data-hint="Use a past form in the imagined condition and would plus the base verb in the present result." data-fix="If I had more time, I would study data analysis.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The mobile test was complete before the launch. By launch day, the team <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="had completed the mobile test" data-hint="Use the Past Perfect for the completed event before the stated past reference point." data-fix="By launch day, the team had completed the mobile test.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The past participle of <span class="ex">write</span> is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="written" data-hint="Recover the three-part sequence write, wrote, written." data-fix="Write, wrote, written.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>At 4:00, the record showed a completed quantity, not only an activity in progress: The team <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="had tested twelve devices" data-hint="A completed number before a past checkpoint favors the Past Perfect Simple." data-fix="By 4:00, the team had tested twelve devices.">choose</button> by 4:00.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice one actual timeline and three unreal relationships</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Fictional Greenline booking launch</h3>
    <p><b>Actual record:</b> The team tested the new booking system on current desktop browsers. It did not test older mobile phones before the May 3 launch. Some mobile users could not complete bookings. The team rolled the system back at 2:30 p.m. Staff were still processing a booking backlog the next morning.</p>
    <p><b>Past alternative and past possibility:</b> <span class="ex">If the team had tested older phones, it might have detected the mobile problem before launch.</span></p>
    <p><b>Past alternative and present consequence:</b> <span class="ex">If the rollout plan had included an offline booking form, staff would not be processing the backlog now.</span></p>
    <p><b>Continuing characteristic and past opportunity:</b> <span class="ex">If the system were less dependent on vendor approval, the team could have rolled it back sooner yesterday.</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Which sentence keeps both the condition and consequence before now?</li>
      <li>Which sentence connects a missing past preparation to work happening now?</li>
      <li>Which sentence uses a continuing system characteristic to explain a lost past opportunity?</li>
      <li>Which modal avoids claiming that the phone test certainly would have found the problem?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You separated the actual record from a past possibility, present backlog consequence, continuing dependency, and calibrated might claim.">
    <h3>Discovery check: identify the time and reality relationship</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Greenline noticing choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="The team did not test older phones before launch">The team did not test older phones before launch</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="an unreal past condition with an uncertain past result">an unreal past condition with an uncertain past result</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="an unreal past condition with a present result">an unreal past condition with a present result</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="an unreal continuing characteristic with a past result">an unreal continuing characteristic with a past result</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="might have detected">might have detected</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a confirmed future booking">a confirmed future booking</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The actual record establishes that <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="The team did not test older phones before launch" data-hint="Use the explicit testing record, not a counterfactual conclusion." data-fix="The team did not test older phones before launch.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span><span class="ex">If the team had tested older phones, it might have detected the problem</span> contains <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="an unreal past condition with an uncertain past result" data-hint="Both events belong before now, and might keeps the result uncertain." data-fix="The sentence combines an unreal past condition with an uncertain past result.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The sentence about the offline form contains <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="an unreal past condition with a present result" data-hint="The missing plan belongs before launch; processing the backlog is happening now." data-fix="The offline-form sentence links an unreal past condition to a present result.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The vendor-dependency sentence contains <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="an unreal continuing characteristic with a past result" data-hint="The system’s dependency is presented as a continuing quality; the rollback opportunity was yesterday." data-fix="The sentence links an unreal continuing characteristic to a lost past opportunity.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The wording that presents detection as possible rather than guaranteed is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="might have detected" data-hint="Might have marks an uncertain alternative past result." data-fix="Might have detected preserves uncertainty about whether the test would reveal the problem.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Get to the root: locate each event before choosing a pattern</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>A counterfactual conditional builds two layers. The <b>actual layer</b> contains what the speaker accepts as reality. The <b>alternative layer</b> changes one condition and imagines a consequence. The speaker chooses a tense for the condition and another form for the result according to their separate times. Do not begin by asking which numbered conditional you need. First ask: What actually happened? What is unreal? When is the alternative condition? When is its consequence?</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>relationship</th><th>condition</th><th>result</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>unreal past → unreal past</td><td><span class="ex">if + Past Perfect</span></td><td><span class="ex">would / could / might have + participle</span></td><td><span class="ex">If we had tested, we might have found the error.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>unreal past → unreal present</td><td><span class="ex">if + Past Perfect</span></td><td><span class="ex">would / could / might + base</span></td><td><span class="ex">If we had prepared a form, we would have less work now.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>unreal past → activity now</td><td><span class="ex">if + Past Perfect</span></td><td><span class="ex">would / could / might be + -ing</span></td><td><span class="ex">If I had taken that role, I would be working abroad now.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>unreal continuing state → unreal past</td><td><span class="ex">if + past form / were</span></td><td><span class="ex">would / could / might have + participle</span></td><td><span class="ex">If the system were simpler, fewer users would have abandoned it yesterday.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>unreal past → unreal future</td><td><span class="ex">if + Past Perfect</span></td><td><span class="ex">would / could / might + base or be + -ing</span></td><td><span class="ex">If I had accepted the post, I would be moving next month.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line"><b>Boundary:</b> The two common mixed patterns are useful anchors, not a complete list of sentences to memorize. The time expressions and context must support the relationship. A present form is not “mixed” merely because the sentence contains <span class="ex">now</span>, and a past condition does not automatically cause every later fact.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You mapped past-past, past-present, past-current activity, continuing-state-past, past-future, and unsupported relationships from explicit time evidence.">
    <h3>Map the condition time and result time</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Conditional time relationship choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="unreal past to unreal past">unreal past to unreal past</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="unreal past to unreal present">unreal past to unreal present</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="unreal past to an activity happening now">unreal past to an activity happening now</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="unreal continuing state to unreal past">unreal continuing state to unreal past</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="unreal past to unreal future">unreal past to unreal future</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="no supported conditional relationship">no supported conditional relationship</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span><span class="ex">If we had checked the file on Monday, we would have corrected it before Tuesday’s launch.</span> This maps <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="unreal past to unreal past" data-hint="The unreal Monday check and pre-launch correction both belong before now." data-fix="The sentence maps an unreal past condition to an unreal past result.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span><span class="ex">If the team had documented the process, the instructions would be clearer now.</span> This maps <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="unreal past to unreal present" data-hint="Documentation was missing earlier; clearer instructions are imagined now." data-fix="The sentence maps an unreal past condition to an unreal present state.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span><span class="ex">If I had accepted the temporary assignment, I would be training the new team this week.</span> This maps <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="unreal past to an activity happening now" data-hint="The acceptance decision belongs in the past; training is an imagined activity during this week." data-fix="The sentence maps an unreal past choice to an activity happening now.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span><span class="ex">If the approval process were faster, the team could have restored service yesterday.</span> This maps <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="unreal continuing state to unreal past" data-hint="The process is characterized generally; the lost restoration opportunity was yesterday." data-fix="The sentence maps an unreal continuing process characteristic to an unreal past result.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span><span class="ex">If Maya had enrolled last fall, she would graduate next June.</span> This maps <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="unreal past to unreal future" data-hint="The enrollment decision is closed; the imagined graduation lies next June." data-fix="The sentence maps an unreal past choice to an unreal future result.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span><span class="ex">The team skipped one test. Therefore, every later problem in every office happened because of that decision.</span> With no causal evidence, the record supports <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="no supported conditional relationship" data-hint="One missing test does not establish a cause for every later problem in every office." data-fix="The broad causal relationship is unsupported by the stated record.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Build the full form: condition, result, negatives, and questions</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>job</th><th>form</th><th>model</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>positive past condition</td><td><span class="ex">if + subject + had + participle</span></td><td><span class="ex">If they had tested the phone...</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>negative past condition</td><td><span class="ex">if + subject + had not + participle</span></td><td><span class="ex">If they had not rushed...</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>past result</td><td><span class="ex">subject + would / could / might have + participle</span></td><td><span class="ex">...they might have found the error.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>negative past result</td><td><span class="ex">subject + would not have + participle</span></td><td><span class="ex">...users would not have lost their entries.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>information question</td><td><span class="ex">question word + would + subject + have + participle...?</span></td><td><span class="ex">What would the test have revealed?</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>yes-or-no question</td><td><span class="ex">Would + subject + have + participle...?</span></td><td><span class="ex">Would the launch have succeeded if they had waited?</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Past participles carry the lexical meaning: <span class="ex">find, found, found; write, wrote, written; choose, chose, chosen; leave, left, left</span>. The auxiliary chain stays ordered: <span class="ex">would not have been</span>, not <span class="ex">would have not be</span>. Put question order in the result question, but keep statement order inside the <span class="ex">if</span>-clause: <span class="ex">What would you have changed if you had led the review?</span></p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You built positive and negative past conditions, past results, present mixed results, and two question types with the complete auxiliary chain.">
    <h3>Form check: preserve every auxiliary and participle</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Conditional form choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="had tested">had tested</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="had not rushed">had not rushed</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="might have found">might have found</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="would not be processing">would not be processing</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="What would the test have revealed">What would the test have revealed</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Would the launch have succeeded">Would the launch have succeeded</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="would had find">would had find</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Build the positive unreal past condition: If the team <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="had tested" data-hint="After if, use had plus the past participle tested." data-fix="If the team had tested older phones, it might have found the error.">choose</button> older phones, it might have found the error.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Build the negative unreal past condition: If the team <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="had not rushed" data-hint="Place not after had and before the participle rushed." data-fix="If the team had not rushed, it could have expanded the test.">choose</button>, it could have expanded the test.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Mark an uncertain past result: A broader test <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="might have found" data-hint="Use might have plus the participle found for an uncertain alternative past result." data-fix="A broader test might have found the error.">choose</button> the error.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Connect the missing offline form to the current ongoing backlog: Staff <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="would not be processing" data-hint="Use would not be plus -ing for an imagined activity that would not be happening now." data-fix="Staff would not be processing the backlog now.">choose</button> the backlog now.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Ask for the information discovered in the unreal test: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="What would the test have revealed" data-hint="Use What, would, subject, have, and the participle revealed." data-fix="What would the test have revealed if the team had run it?">choose</button> if the team had run it?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>Ask a yes-or-no question about the unreal past result: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Would the launch have succeeded" data-hint="Begin with Would, then subject, have, and the participle succeeded." data-fix="Would the launch have succeeded if the team had waited?">choose</button> if the team had waited?</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Choose the result modal without claiming more than you know</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>result</th><th>speaker’s claim inside the alternative</th><th>model</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">would have + participle</span></td><td>the speaker treats the result as the expected consequence</td><td><span class="ex">With the correct address, the message would have arrived.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">could have + participle</span></td><td>the alternative creates an ability or available opportunity</td><td><span class="ex">With more time, we could have tested another device.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">might have + participle</span></td><td>the result remains possible and uncertain</td><td><span class="ex">A larger test might have revealed the bug.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">would / could / might + base</span></td><td>the consequence belongs now or later, with the same modal contrast</td><td><span class="ex">With an offline form, staff would have less work now.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><span class="ex">Would</span> expresses the speaker’s expected consequence inside the counterfactual model. It is not scientific proof that the cause is sufficient. If several intervening factors are unknown, <span class="ex">might</span> may be more honest. Use <span class="ex">could</span> for an option or capacity, not merely as a more sophisticated replacement for every <span class="ex">would</span>.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You matched expected consequence, available opportunity, uncertain result, negative expected result, and uncertain present consequence to the stated evidence.">
    <h3>Meaning check: calibrate the alternative result</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Conditional modal result choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="would have arrived">would have arrived</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="could have tested">could have tested</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="might have revealed">might have revealed</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="would not have lost">would not have lost</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="might be using">might be using</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="definitely caused every result">definitely caused every result</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The address was the only documented delivery problem, and a corrected simulation delivered every copy. With the correct address, the message <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="would have arrived" data-hint="The supplied simulation supports the expected alternative result." data-fix="With the correct address, the message would have arrived.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The extra two hours would have created an opportunity for one more device test, but the result is unknown: The team <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="could have tested" data-hint="Could have expresses the available opportunity created by extra time." data-fix="With two more hours, the team could have tested another device.">choose</button> another device.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>No one reran the missing phone test, so detection remains uncertain: The test <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="might have revealed" data-hint="Might have preserves uncertainty about what the unperformed test would show." data-fix="The missing phone test might have revealed the bug.">choose</button> the bug.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The log confirms that the broken save action erased the entries. With a working save action, users <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="would not have lost" data-hint="The controlled log comparison supports the expected negative alternative result." data-fix="With a working save action, users would not have lost their entries.">choose</button> their entries.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The company declined an analytics tool last year, but several other adoption factors remain unknown. With that tool, the team <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="might be using" data-hint="Use might for an uncertain present consequence of the past decision." data-fix="If the company had bought the tool, the team might be using better forecasts now.">choose</button> better forecasts now.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Keep counterfactual analysis separate from proof and blame</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>A counterfactual asks what might differ if one part of reality changed. Good analysis states the known sequence, identifies the changed condition, explains the proposed mechanism, calibrates the result, and names relevant uncertainty. Hindsight can reveal a useful missed safeguard, but later knowledge does not prove that a reasonable person should have predicted the exact outcome earlier.</p>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Four levels of claim</h3>
    <p><b>Known fact:</b> <span class="ex">The team did not test older phones before launch.</span></p>
    <p><b>Available opportunity:</b> <span class="ex">With two more hours, the team could have tested three older phones.</span></p>
    <p><b>Uncertain outcome:</b> <span class="ex">That test might have revealed the save problem.</span></p>
    <p><b>Unsupported blame:</b> <span class="ex">If the tester had cared, no problem would ever have occurred.</span> The record does not establish carelessness, complete prevention, or one person’s control over every outcome.</p>
  </div>
  <p><span class="ex">Should have + participle</span> can evaluate a past action: <span class="ex">The plan should have included a mobile test.</span> It is not itself a conditional. Add a conditional only when you need the alternative consequence: <span class="ex">If the plan had included that test, the team might have found the problem.</span></p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You separated record, opportunity, uncertain outcome, unsupported blame, and should-have evaluation from the conditional consequence.">
    <h3>Evidence check: identify the kind of claim</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Counterfactual evidence choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="known fact">known fact</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="available opportunity">available opportunity</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="uncertain counterfactual outcome">uncertain counterfactual outcome</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="unsupported blame">unsupported blame</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="past evaluation without a stated conditional result">past evaluation without a stated conditional result</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="proof of every alternative world">proof of every alternative world</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span><span class="ex">The test record contains current desktops but no older phones.</span> This is a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="known fact" data-hint="The sentence reports what the supplied record contains and omits." data-fix="The device list is a known fact in the record.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span><span class="ex">With two more hours, the team could have tested three older phones.</span> This describes an <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="available opportunity" data-hint="Could have presents what extra time would make possible, not what the test result would be." data-fix="The sentence describes an available opportunity in the alternative situation.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span><span class="ex">The additional test might have revealed the save problem.</span> This is an <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="uncertain counterfactual outcome" data-hint="The test never happened, and might preserves uncertainty about its result." data-fix="The sentence presents an uncertain counterfactual outcome.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span><span class="ex">If the tester had cared, no problem would ever have occurred.</span> With no evidence about motivation or total control, this is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="unsupported blame" data-hint="The record does not establish the tester’s attitude or complete prevention." data-fix="The sentence makes an unsupported blame claim.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span><span class="ex">The plan should have included a mobile test.</span> This is a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="past evaluation without a stated conditional result" data-hint="Should have evaluates the plan, but no if-clause or alternative consequence is stated." data-fix="Should have included is a past evaluation without a stated conditional result.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Use standard counterfactual if-clauses and recognize formal inversion</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>For a standard unreal past condition, use the Past Perfect in the <span class="ex">if</span>-clause: <span class="ex">If we had known, we would have acted.</span> Avoid <span class="ex">if we would have known</span> in careful standard English. Forms with <span class="ex">would have</span> in the <span class="ex">if</span>-clause occur in some informal varieties, but they are not the target pattern for formal, academic, or professional counterfactual analysis.</p>
  <p><span class="ex">Would</span> can appear in an <span class="ex">if</span>-clause for a different meaning, such as willingness or a formal request: <span class="ex">If you would wait here, the coordinator will speak with you.</span> That is not a Third Conditional. The presence of <span class="ex">if</span> does not give every <span class="ex">would</span> the same job.</p>
  <p>Formal writing can remove <span class="ex">if</span> and invert <span class="ex">had</span> with the subject: <span class="ex">Had the team tested older phones, it might have found the problem.</span> For a negative condition: <span class="ex">Had the team not restored the backup, more records would have been lost.</span> This is a statement, not a question. Use it selectively; repeated inversion can sound unnecessarily formal.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You used the standard Past Perfect if-clause, separated willingness, recognized positive and negative inversion, and retained natural register.">
    <h3>Usage check: choose form by meaning and register</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="If clause and inversion choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="If we had known, we would have changed the plan">If we had known, we would have changed the plan</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a formal request for willingness">a formal request for willingness</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Had the team tested older phones">Had the team tested older phones</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Had the team not restored the backup">Had the team not restored the backup</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="use inversion selectively in formal writing">use inversion selectively in formal writing</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="If we would have knew">If we would have knew</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Use the careful standard counterfactual pattern: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="If we had known, we would have changed the plan" data-hint="Put had known in the if-clause and would have changed in the result." data-fix="If we had known, we would have changed the plan.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>In <span class="ex">If you would wait here, the coordinator will speak with you</span>, the if-clause expresses <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a formal request for willingness" data-hint="Would asks whether the listener is willing to wait; it does not build an unreal past condition." data-fix="Would wait expresses a formal request for willingness here.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Remove <span class="ex">if</span> from <span class="ex">If the team had tested older phones...</span>: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Had the team tested older phones" data-hint="Move had before the subject and remove if; keep the participle tested." data-fix="Had the team tested older phones, it might have found the problem.">choose</button>, it might have found the problem.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Build the formal negative inversion: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Had the team not restored the backup" data-hint="Use Had plus subject plus not plus participle." data-fix="Had the team not restored the backup, more records would have been lost.">choose</button>, more records would have been lost.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>For a clear professional report, the best register guidance is to <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="use inversion selectively in formal writing" data-hint="Inversion is available but can sound overly formal when repeated." data-fix="Use conditional inversion selectively in formal writing.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Sound natural: hear <span class="ex">had</span>, <span class="ex">would have</span>, and the contrast</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p><span class="ex">Had</span> and <span class="ex">would</span> both contract to <span class="ex">’d</span> after a subject. The following verb form reveals the job: <span class="ex">We’d TESTed it</span> means <span class="ex">we had tested it</span>; <span class="ex">we’d TEST it</span> means <span class="ex">we would test it</span>. In a Third Conditional, <span class="ex">If we’d KNOWN, / we would’ve ACTed</span> contrasts past <span class="ex">had known</span> with result <span class="ex">would have acted</span>.</p>
  <p>In relaxed U.S. speech, <span class="ex">would have</span> often becomes <span class="ex">would’ve</span> /ˈwʊdəv/, <span class="ex">could have</span> becomes <span class="ex">could’ve</span> /ˈkʊdəv/, and <span class="ex">might have</span> becomes <span class="ex">might’ve</span> /ˈmaɪtəv/. The final sound is a weak form of <span class="ex">have</span>, not the word <span class="ex">of</span>. Write <span class="ex">would have</span> or <span class="ex">would’ve</span>, never <span class="ex">would of</span>.</p>
  <p>Use thought groups to keep the two worlds clear: <span class="ex">If we’d tested the OLD phones, / we MIGHT have found the BUG.</span> Contrastive stress can correct one part: <span class="ex">We might have found the MOBILE problem, / not the PAYMENT problem.</span> In a mixed conditional, make the time shift audible: <span class="ex">If we’d prepared an offline FORM, / staff wouldn’t be processing the backlog NOW.</span></p>
  <section data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read had-versus-would contractions, weak have, modal result meaning, thought groups, and mixed-time listening check">
